From df80b7dc842c9219f7b9340a70c9b07471de1e9f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: skornuta <126527569+skornuta@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 12:26:23 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 04/27] Memory minute: a row a minute of where the process's
 memory is

The resident sampler (every 30 s, on its own thread) now hands every other
pass to `ramline::minute`, which logs a "memory minute" line and appends a
row to `<cache>\memory.csv` (`VICTORY_MEMORY_CSV=<path>`, `=0`: none),
headed with the run's VICTORY_ switches. Beside the process and system
counts (private, peak, working set, free, system commit and limit) and
the card's (budget and use, both segments) it names what we track - title
memory by kind, host copies' CPU sides, staging, the snapshot pool, the
Rust heap, our CPU-visible D3D12 buffers - and what is left:
`unattributed_private`, the private memory no category names (the
driver's own command memory among it), and `sysmem_gpu_untracked`, what
the system counts as shared with the card beyond our buffers.

- Rust heap: `heapcount` is striped, a counter on a cache line per thread
  (64), summed by the sampler; on unless `VICTORY_HEAP_COUNT=0`. The old
  shared counter slowed loading and was off, so release builds said 0.
- Our D3D12 buffers: `memtel::track` counts what `upload_buffer`,
  `shared_buffer` and `gpu_upload_buffer` make, and D3D12's destruction
  callback (`ID3DDestructionNotifier`) takes it off, whoever let it go.

Nothing else changes. Tests: stripes sum exactly with blocks freed on
other threads; a tracked buffer counts until D3D12 destroys it (WARP).

(cherry picked from commit f879d92f556b6ac01237390d026a74479fce7cf3)
